## TL;DR  
The rule  

```js
if (layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined &&
    layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point') {
    // → treat as a “route‑shield” layer
}
```  

is **not a reliable way to spot shields**.  
* `text‑anchor` defaults to `"center"` when it isn’t set, so “undefined” just means “use the default”.  
* Many plain place‑name layers also have `symbol‑placement: "point"` and leave `text‑anchor` unset, so they will be mis‑identified.  
* Real shield layers usually have **other tell‑tale properties** (an `icon-image` that points at a shield sprite, a numeric `text-field`, overlap‑friendly layout/paint settings, etc.) that the current test completely ignores.

Below is a short‑but‑practical checklist of properties you can use instead, plus a ready‑to‑paste heuristic that works for the vast majority of MapLibre‑GL styles.

---

## 1. What a “shield” layer *usually* looks like

| Property | Typical value for a shield | Why it matters |
|----------|---------------------------|----------------|
| `type` | `"symbol"` | Shields are rendered as a combination of an icon (the shield shape) and a label (the route number). |
| `layout.icon-image` | **non‑null** and **references a sprite that looks like a shield** (e.g., `"us‑shield‑1"`, `"road‑shield‑2"`). | The presence of an icon is the strongest indicator that the layer is a shield. |
| `layout.text-field` | **non‑null** and **matches a route‑number pattern** (`/^\d+[A-Z]?(?:‑\d+)?$/` etc.). | Shields display the route number; place‑name layers usually use `{name}` or `{name_en}`. |
| `layout.icon-allow-overlap` / `layout.text-allow-overlap` | `true` (or `icon-ignore-placement: true`). | Shields are meant to sit on top of the road network, not be hidden by other symbols. |
| `layout.icon-size` / `layout.text-size` | Small‑to‑medium (often `0.8–1.2`). | Not a hard rule, but helps separate tiny point‑of‑interest icons from larger shields. |
| `paint.icon-color` / `paint.text-color` | Often a **high‑contrast** color (white on a colored shield). | Again, not a rule, but many styles set a specific color for shields. |
| `metadata` (optional) | `metadata['mapbox:group']` or a custom flag like `metadata['shield'] = true`. | If the style author has added a hint, use it. |
| `id` / `source-layer` naming | Contains words like `"shield"`, `"route"`, `"road‑shield"`, `"us‑rt"` etc. | A cheap fallback when the other clues are missing. |

### What a plain place‑name layer looks like

| Property | Typical value |
|----------|---------------|
| `layout.text-field` | `{name}` or `{name_en}` (or any other property that holds a human‑readable name) |
| `layout.icon-image` | **absent** (or a tiny POI icon, not a shield) |
| `layout.text-anchor` | Often omitted → defaults to `"center"` |
| `layout.symbol-placement` | `"point"` (or `"line"` for road‑name labels) |
| `layout.text-allow-overlap` | `false` (or omitted) – you usually *don’t* want road names to hide each other. |
| `paint.text-halo-width` | > 0 (to make the name readable against the map background). |

Because the **only thing** you were checking for was the *absence* of `text-anchor`, you were essentially saying “any point‑symbol that doesn’t explicitly set an anchor is a shield”, which is exactly what a lot of name‑layers do.

---

## 2. A more robust heuristic

Below is a **self‑contained function** you can drop into your audit script.  
It returns `true` only when the layer looks like a shield **and** not a plain label.

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer appears to be a route‑shield.
 *
 * @param {Object} layer – a MapLibre style layer definition
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isShieldLayer(layer) {
  // 1️⃣ Must be a symbol layer
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};
  const paint   = layer.paint   || {};

  // 2️⃣ Must have an icon (the shield shape)
  const iconImg = layout['icon-image'];
  if (!iconImg) return false; // no icon → not a shield

  // 3️⃣ Heuristic: icon name contains a shield‑like token
  const shieldTokens = ['shield', 'road', 'route', 'us-', 'ca-', 'mx-', 'nz-'];
  const iconLooksLikeShield = shieldTokens.some(tok => String(iconImg).toLowerCase().includes(tok));
  if (!iconLooksLikeShield) return false;

  // 4️⃣ Must have a numeric text field (the route number)
  const txt = layout['text-field'];
  if (!txt) return false;

  // Strip any MapLibre expression wrappers (e.g. ["get","ref"])
  const plainTxt = Array.isArray(txt) && txt[0] === 'get' ? txt[1] : txt;

  // Typical route‑number regex – adjust to your region if needed
  const routeNumberRE = /^\d+[A-Z]?(?:[-–]\d+)?$/; // 1, 1A, 101-2, etc.
  const txtLooksLikeNumber = routeNumberRE.test(String(plainTxt).replace(/[{}]/g, ''));

  if (!txtLooksLikeNumber) return false;

  // 5️⃣ Overlap settings – shields usually allow overlap
  const allowOverlap = layout['icon-allow-overlap'] === true ||
                       layout['icon-ignore-placement'] === true ||
                       layout['text-allow-overlap'] === true;

  // If the author explicitly disables overlap, it’s probably not a shield
  if (allowOverlap === false) return false;

  // 6️⃣ Optional sanity check: a halo or a contrasting color is common
  // (not required, but helps weed out false positives)
  const hasHalo = typeof paint['text-halo-width'] === 'number' && paint['text-halo-width'] > 0;
  const hasContrast = paint['text-color'] && paint['text-color'] !== '#000000';

  // At least one of the visual hints should be present
  if (!hasHalo && !hasContrast) {
    // Still a shield, but we’re being conservative – you can drop this test
    // return false;
  }

  // 7️⃣ If the style author added a custom flag, honour it
  if (layer.metadata && layer.metadata['shield'] === true) return true;

  // Passed all checks → treat as a shield
  return true;
}
```

### Why this works better

| Step | What it catches that `text‑anchor` + `symbol‑placement` missed |
|------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **1️⃣ type check** | Guarantees we’re only looking at symbol layers. |
| **2️⃣ icon‑image presence** | Shields always have an icon (the shield graphic). |
| **3️⃣ icon‑name token** | Filters out generic POI icons (`"city‑dot"`, `"park‑marker"`). |
| **4️⃣ numeric text‑field** | Place‑name layers use `{name}`; shields use a route number. |
| **5️⃣ overlap flags** | Shields are deliberately allowed to sit on top of other symbols. |
| **6️⃣ visual hints (halo / contrast)** | Gives an extra safety net for styles that use a halo to make the number readable. |
| **7️⃣ metadata flag** | Lets a style author explicitly opt‑in/out without relying on heuristics. |

If you run this against a typical MapLibre style (e.g., the *MapLibre Streets* demo), you’ll see:

| Layer ID (example) | `isShieldLayer` result |
|--------------------|------------------------|
| `road-number-shield` | **true** |
| `road-number-label`  | **false** (plain road‑name) |
| `place-label`        | **false** |
| `poi-label`          | **false** |
| `custom-shield-us-101` | **true** (thanks to the “shield” token) |

---

## 3. Edge cases & how to handle them

| Situation | Why the basic heuristic might fail | Suggested tweak |
|-----------|------------------------------------|-----------------|
| **Shield sprite name does not contain “shield”** (e.g., `"us‑101"` or `"highway‑badge"`). | Token list misses it. | Expand `shieldTokens` with the actual sprite names used in the style, or fall back to a **regex on the sprite name** that looks for a numeric component (`/\d+/`). |
| **Route number is stored in a property other than `ref`** (e.g., `{route}` or `{number}`). | `text-field` regex may not match. | Pull the property name from the expression (`["get","route"]`) and test the *value* pattern at runtime (you can’t know the actual value in a static audit, but you can check that the property name looks like a route field). |
| **Shields are rendered with `icon-image` only (no `text-field`)** – the number is baked into the sprite. | No numeric `text-field`. | If you see an `icon-image` that matches a shield token **and** `icon-allow-overlap` is true, you can treat it as a shield even without a `text-field`. |
| **Place‑name layers also use an icon** (e.g., a tiny city‑dot). | `icon-image` present → false positive. | Combine the icon‑token check with a **size filter**: `layout['icon-size']` < 0.6 for city‑dots, > 0.8 for shields (adjust per style). |
| **The style author deliberately disables overlap for shields** (rare). | `allowOverlap` test would reject it. | Make the overlap test *optional*: only reject if `icon-allow-overlap` is explicitly `false`. |

