### TL;DR  
The rule  

```js
layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined &&
layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point'
```  

is **not a reliable way to spot a route‑shield**.  
Almost every point‑symbol (city names, POIs, transit stops, etc.) satisfies those two conditions, so you’ll get a lot of false‑positives and you’ll miss the real shields that *do* set a `text‑anchor` (or that use a separate icon‑only layer).

A more robust heuristic is to look at **what makes a shield a shield**:

| What a shield usually has | Why it matters |
|---------------------------|----------------|
| **`icon-image`** that points to a sprite named something like `shield‑*`, `road‑shield`, `route‑*`, etc. | The graphic background of the shield lives in the sprite sheet. |
| **`text-field`** (or a companion text‑only layer) that contains a *route number* (e.g., `“101”`, `“I‑5”`, `“A12”`). | The alphanumeric label that sits on top of the icon. |
| **`text-optional: true`** (or a separate text‑only layer) – shields often allow the text to be omitted when the icon can’t be drawn. |
| **`icon-allow-overlap: true`** and **`text-allow-overlap: true`** (or very high `icon-ignore-placement`) – shields need to sit on top of other symbols. |
| **`symbol-placement: 'point'`** – shields are point‑placed, but this alone isn’t enough. |
| **`text-font` / `text-size`** that look like a typical shield style (e.g., a bold sans‑serif at 10‑12 px). | Gives you a secondary clue when the sprite name is generic. |
| **Metadata** (`layer.metadata['mapbox:group']`, `layer.metadata['mapbox:shield']`, `layer.metadata['maplibre:shield']`, etc.) – some style authors add a custom flag. | If present, it’s the cleanest signal. |

Below is a practical, step‑by‑step recipe you can embed in your audit script.

---

## 1. Why the current heuristic fails

```js
layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined
```

* The default for `text-anchor` is `"center"`. If the author never set it, the property is simply omitted – **the same thing the spec does for *any* symbol layer**.

```js
layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point'
```

* Almost every point‑based label (city names, POIs, transit stops, etc.) uses `symbol-placement: 'point'`.  

Result: **every plain place‑name layer looks like a shield**.

---

## 2. What a “real” shield looks like in a MapLibre style

A typical shield is built from **two layers** (though a single layer works too):

```json
{
  "id": "road-shield-bg",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "osm",
  "source-layer": "road",
  "filter": ["==", "$type", "LineString"],
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "road-shield-1",   // sprite name
    "icon-size": 0.8,
    "symbol-placement": "point",
    "icon-allow-overlap": true,
    "icon-ignore-placement": true
  }
},
{
  "id": "road-shield-label",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "osm",
  "source-layer": "road",
  "filter": ["==", "$type", "LineString"],
  "layout": {
    "text-field": "{ref}",           // route number from data
    "text-font": ["Open Sans Bold"],
    "text-size": 10,
    "text-optional": true,
    "symbol-placement": "point",
    "text-allow-overlap": true,
    "text-ignore-placement": true
  }
}
```

Key take‑aways:

* **`icon-image` is present** (the shield graphic).  
* **`text-field` is present** (the route number).  
* Both layers use `symbol-placement: 'point'` **and** set overlap‑allowing flags.  
* The `text-anchor` is often explicitly set to `"center"` (or omitted, but that’s fine because we already have the icon).

---

## 3. A more reliable heuristic

Below is a **pure‑JavaScript** function you can drop into your audit script.  
It tries to identify a shield **by the combination of icon + route‑like text** and, if those are missing, falls back to a sprite‑name pattern.

```js
/**
 * Returns true if the given layer looks like a route shield.
 *
 * @param {Object} layer – a MapLibre style layer definition
 * @param {Object} [options] – optional tuning parameters
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isShieldLayer(layer, options = {}) {
  // 1️⃣ Must be a symbol layer
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};
  const paint   = layer.paint   || {};

  // 2️⃣ Must be point‑placed (most shields are)
  if (layout['symbol-placement'] !== 'point') return false;

  // 3️⃣ Look for a route‑like text field (e.g. "101", "I‑5", "A12")
  const textField = layout['text-field'];
  const looksLikeRoute = typeof textField === 'string' &&
    // simple regex that matches typical route identifiers
    /^[A-Z]?\d{1,4}[A-Z]?(?:[-–]\d+)?$/i.test(textField.replace(/[{}]/g, ''));

  // 4️⃣ Look for an icon that *could* be a shield sprite
  const iconImage = layout['icon-image'];
  const iconLooksLikeShield = typeof iconImage === 'string' &&
    // common naming conventions – adjust to your own sprite set
    /(^|[-_])shield|road[-_]shield|route[-_]icon|sign[-_]shield/i.test(iconImage);

  // 5️⃣ Overlap flags – shields usually allow overlap
  const allowOverlap = layout['icon-allow-overlap'] === true ||
                       layout['text-allow-overlap'] === true ||
                       layout['icon-ignore-placement'] === true ||
                       layout['text-ignore-placement'] === true;

  // 6️⃣ Optional: custom metadata flag (if your style author uses it)
  const metaFlag = layer.metadata && (
    layer.metadata['mapbox:shield'] ||
    layer.metadata['maplibre:shield'] ||
    layer.metadata['shield']
  );

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // Decision tree
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // a) Explicit metadata flag wins outright
  if (metaFlag) return true;

  // b) If we have BOTH an icon and a route‑like text, we’re almost certainly a shield
  if (iconLooksLikeShield && looksLikeRoute) return true;

  // c) If we have an icon that looks like a shield *and* the layer is allowed to overlap,
  //    treat it as a shield even if the text is missing (some styles draw the number
  //    in the sprite itself)
  if (iconLooksLikeShield && allowOverlap) return true;

  // d) If we have a route‑like text *and* the layer is allowed to overlap,
  //    it’s probably a shield‑only text layer (icon is in a separate layer)
  if (looksLikeRoute && allowOverlap) return true;

  // e) Nothing matched → not a shield
  return false;
}
```

### How the function works

| Step | What it checks | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|----------------|
| **1** | `layer.type === 'symbol'` | Shields are always symbol layers. |
| **2** | `symbol-placement === 'point'` | Shields are anchored to a point (the road centreline). |
| **3** | `text-field` matches a route‑number regex | The *label* on a shield is almost always a route identifier. |
| **4** | `icon-image` contains “shield”, “road‑shield”, etc. | The background graphic lives in the sprite sheet. |
| **5** | Overlap‑allowing flags (`icon-allow-overlap`, `text-allow-overlap`, `icon-ignore-placement`, `text-ignore-placement`) | Shields need to sit on top of other symbols; most styles turn overlap on for them. |
| **6** | `metadata['*shield*']` | Some style authors add an explicit flag – use it if present. |

You can tweak the regexes or the sprite‑name pattern to match the naming conventions of the particular style you’re auditing.

---

## 4. Handling the “two‑layer” pattern

Many styles **split the shield into two layers**:

1. **Background icon layer** – only `icon-image` (no `text-field`).  
2. **Label layer** – only `text-field` (no `icon-image`).

If you want to treat the pair as a single “shield” entity, you can run the heuristic on each layer **and then group** them by a common identifier (e.g., same `source-layer` and a matching filter, or a naming convention like `*_bg` / `*_label`).  

A quick way to catch the pair:

```js
function isShieldPair(layers) {
  const icons = layers.filter(l => l.type === 'symbol' && l.layout?.['icon-image']);
  const texts = layers.filter(l => l.type === 'symbol' && l.layout?.['text-field']);

  // Look for an icon layer whose sprite name looks like a shield
  // and a text layer whose field looks like a route number,
  // both sharing the same source & source-layer.
  for (const icon of icons) {
    const src = icon.source;
    const srcLayer = icon['source-layer'];
    const iconLooks = /shield|road[-_]shield|route[-_]icon/i.test(icon.layout['icon-image']);

    if (!iconLooks) continue;

    const matchingText = texts.find(t =>
      t.source === src &&
      t['source-layer'] === srcLayer &&
      typeof t.layout?.['text-field'] === 'string' &&
      /^[A-Z]?\d{1,4}[A-Z]?(?:[-–]\d+)?$/i.test(t.layout['text-field'].replace(/[{}]/g, ''))
    );

    if (matchingText) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

You can call `isShieldPair(style.layers)` after you’ve filtered out any layers that already returned `true` from `isShieldLayer`.

