## 5. Edge Cases & Tips

| Situation | Why the simple heuristic can still fail | How to mitigate |
|-----------|------------------------------------------|-----------------|
| **Shield sprites are named generically** (e.g., `icon-1`, `icon-2`) | No “shield” keyword to match. | Rely more on the **route‑like `text-field`** and the overlap flags. |
| **Route numbers are stored in a property other than `ref`** (e.g., `route_number`) | The `text-field` may be `{route_number}` which still matches the regex, but you need to be sure the property exists. | Optionally inspect the source data

### TL;DR  
The “`text‑anchor === undefined && symbol‑placement === 'point'`” test is **not a reliable way to spot route‑shield symbols**.  
A lot of ordinary point‑labels (city names, POIs, etc.) satisfy exactly the same conditions, while many genuine shields do **not** (they often set `text-anchor`, use a data‑driven `icon-image`, or rely on other layout/paint properties).  

A much sturdier heuristic is to look for the **combination of an icon (the shield graphic) and a text field (the route number)**, plus a few ancillary clues that are typical of shield layers:

| What to check | Why it helps | Typical values / patterns |
|---------------|--------------|---------------------------|
| `layer.type === 'symbol'` | Shields are always symbol layers. | – |
| **`layout['icon-image']` is defined** (string or expression) | The shield graphic lives in the sprite sheet. | `"road-shield-{ref}"`, `"shield-{ref}"`, `"highway-{class}"`, etc. |
| **`layout['text-field']` is defined** (string or expression) | The route number (or other identifier) is rendered on top of the graphic. | `"{ref}"`, `"{ref}-{suffix}"`, `"{route}"`, `"{number}"` |
| **`layout['icon-allow-overlap'] === true`** (or omitted – defaults to `false` for most labels) | Shields are usually allowed to overlap other symbols because they’re drawn on top of the road line. | `true` |
| **`layout['text-allow-overlap'] === true`** (or omitted) | Same reasoning as above for the numeric label. | `true` |
| **`layout['icon-size']` (or a data‑driven expression)** | Shields are often scaled relative to the map zoom or to a property like `ref_length`. | `1.5`, `['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'], 10, 0.8, 14, 1.2]` |
| **`layout['icon-pitch-alignment']` / `layout['icon-rotation-alignment']`** set to `"map"` | Shields stay upright on the map surface. | `"map"` |
| **Sprite‑name pattern** – if you have access to the sprite sheet, look for names that contain “shield”, “road‑shield”, “route‑”, “highway‑”, etc. | Gives you a whitelist of known shield graphics. | `road-shield-1`, `highway-2`, `us‑101` |
| **`metadata`** – some style authors add a custom `metadata` block (e.g., `metadata: { shield: true }`). | If present, it’s the cleanest signal. | – |

If you can’t rely on sprite‑name conventions (e.g., the style uses generic icons), the **presence of both an `icon-image` and a `text-field` that reference the same data property** (`ref`, `route`, `number`, etc.) is usually enough to separate shields from plain place‑name labels.

